WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:08.140
Over the last year, several states have passed controversial laws restricting abortion.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.360
Laws passed in Alabama and Georgia that would effectively ban the procedure

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:18.020
are aimed at giving the nation's high court an opportunity to reverse its controversial 1973 ruling

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.020
in Roe vs. Wade.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.880
The history of criminalization pre-Roe

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.040
provides a window into how abortion bans will play out if re-instituted,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.420
and points to ways of reducing the number of abortions in the U.S.,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.760
while protecting choice and reproductive health.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.620
Before the passage of Roe vs. Wade, government prohibition wasn't very effective.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.560
After the first state laws criminalizing abortion were passed in the late 19th century,

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.380
women found ways to obtain the procedure anyway,

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.340
even with the threat of arrest and prosecution hanging over their heads.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.920
Upper-class white women were more likely to find relatively safe ways to break the law,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.520
while everyone else turned to shady operators risking infertility and death.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.960
There is no need to speculate about what would happen if full-scale abortion bans were restored.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.460
All we have to do is look to the past.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.380
Abortion was not always a hot-button political issue in American life.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:20.000
Before the 1850's local papers from Kansas to New York had ads for female pills promising relief for ladies.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.940
Female physicians, like Madam Rustell,  solicited clients looking to be treated for obstruction

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.660
of the monthly period.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.380
But the use of coded language merely reflected the Victorian-era sensibilities.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.580
Up until the 19th century, abortion of early pregnancy was legal and common.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.220
These abortion ads stirred little controversy.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:45.040
Bearing children was risky. Women were about 40 times more likely to die giving birth than today.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.780
The infant mortality rate in the late 1800's was over 17 percent.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.560
Abortive tonics and potions were generally ineffective and potentially dangerous,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.680
but determined women could turn to midwives or physicians for procedures

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.600
that roughly mirrored modern techniques.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.200
When Abortion was a Crime, by University of Illinois historian Leslie Reagan,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.180
documents how terminating pregnancies was both common practice and an open secret.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.140
An estimated one in five women had an abortion during this period,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.900
and they tended to be married and upper-class.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.660
Prevalent cultural attitudes of the time only viewed abortion after the first trimester as immoral.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.440
For the 1850's the turning point from legal to illegal was known as "the quickening,"

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.640
or when a woman could feel the fetus move, which generally happens in the second trimester.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.200
Quickening was an important distinction under common law and Catholic doctrine.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.400
Public sentiment shifted in the second half of the 19th century.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.960
The criminalization of abortion began mainly as a business tactic to bring women's reproductive health

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.080
under control of the emerging physician class.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.780
Accredited male doctors campaign to pass abortion bans at the state and federal level

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.920
as a way to shut down unlicensed and mostly female practitioners.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.040
The end goal to establish state control over the medical profession.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.820
It was the newly formed American Medical Association,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:14.020
under the direction of Dr.Horacio Storer that led the effort and criminalizing abortion.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:17.380
Storer made a passionate moral argument against the procedure,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.760
which had the effect of de-legitimizing expert female practitioners.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.040
Again media coverage skewed the reality of abortion procedures.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.740
The death rate from abortions performed by midwives and physicians were nearly identical,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.680
yet news reports blamed non-accredited female practitioners

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.680
with sensationalized stories of women bleeding to death from botched procedures.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.200
Storer also capitalized on fears of falling birth rates among white native-born women,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.200
arguing that if they were allowed to abort,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.360
it would hasten the takeover of the country by foreigners and people of color.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:56.060
In 1894, then New York City Police Commissioner Teddy Roosevelt, called white women who sought abortions

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.380
"race criminals."

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.240
The American Medical
Association's efforts were a resounding success,

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.540
and driving the practice underground.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:09.640
By 1880, most states had passed laws that prohibited abortion except when a woman's life was in danger.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.680
And generally male doctors got to make the decision when it came to a woman's personal health.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.400
The American Medical Association also scored a victory at the federal level,

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.280
with the passage of the 1873 Comstock law,

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.640
an anti-obscenity bill that prohibited sending abortion and birth control information

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.180
through the U.S. Postal Service.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.720
While abortions were illegal, most women were still able to obtain one sympathetic physicians and midwives

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.440
who tended to disregard the law and comply with their patient's request.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.360
Over more than a century of criminalization,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.320
from after the Civil War to the passage of
Roe vs. Wade,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.040
scholars estimate that a quarter of all pregnancies ended in abortion.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.680
Another consequence of abortion bans was law enforcement interfering in private lives.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.120
Cops fought back against illegal abortions with arrests, interrogations, and prosecution.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.160
Police officers and prosecutors threatened doctors with jail time

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.740
to get them to collaborate with local law enforcement,

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.140
and punishing women who sought their services.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.920
In 1902, the Journal of the American Medical Association

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.620
advised physicians to demand bedside statements of exoneration

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.480
before treating women who had undergone botched abortions.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.440
Men whose lovers had died while having a pregnancy terminated

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.000
were sometimes arrested and charged with failing to fulfill their paternal obligations.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.420
By the Great Depression, abortion was illegal in every state, and yet the practice had become more common.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.940
Medical studies and sex surveys indicated that women of every social class

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.780
turned to abortion in greater numbers as the economy deteriorated.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.340
But only the wealthy and well-connected
could safely obtain the procedure

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.380
through the loophole of medical necessity.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:56.600
This generally involved paying out of pocket to be examined by three doctors, including a psychiatrist,

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.100
who would make a recommendation to a hospital board.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.700
Women who couldn't afford this option turned to illegal providers

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.260
whose methods brought the risk of permanent infertility or death.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.000
The crackdown on abortion intensified after World War Two.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.540
Physicians joined hospitals in clinics and mass, which brought institutional scrutiny of their decisions.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.240
Stage tried to close the loophole exploited by wealthy women

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.140
by mandating approval from a hospital appointed committee.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:31.720
Police raided abortion facilities and then physically examined the arrestees while in custody.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:36.220
Getting a safe abortion became harder, so
the practice went further underground.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.980
Organizations like the clergy consultation service,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.360
founded by a group of Protestant ministers and Jewish
rabbis,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.760
ferried women to states where abortion was legal.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:52.080
By 1970, the group was helping an estimated 150,000 women a year terminate their pregnancies.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.200
Other groups helped women travel to Mexico or Puerto Rico to have the procedure done safely.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.360
Women who cannot access reliable services turned to nefarious, untrained practitioners.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.080
The result was a spike in death rates from abortions in the 1960s.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:12.740
Researchers at UCLA predicted that criminalization would result in the deaths of 5,000 women each year

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.200
during this time period.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:23.220
Hospitalizations from botched abortions became a national epidemic.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:28.060
Treating women who had abortion complications helped convince a segment of the medical community,

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:31.720
that once called for restrictive bans, to push for decriminalization.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:37.460
The professions about face set the stage for the Supreme Court's 1973 decision  in Roe vs. Wade,

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:42.000
which struck down state laws criminalizing abortion as unconstitutional.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.340
Today that decision could be overturned by the high court's conservative majority.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:50.440
If that happens it will not only once again drive the practice underground,

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.000
but also results in a whole new realm of state surveillance and misapplication of laws

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.840
to monitor women's bodies.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.140
As with prohibition of drugs, alcohol, and sex work,

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:06.100
a legal ban on abortion wouldn't be effective because so many women don't believe the practice should be illegal.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.860
But they also have an easier time than ever before of not getting pregnant in the first place.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.200
One of the biggest changes since the days when abortion first became a crime in the 19th century,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.920
is better birth control.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.540
Today wider access to contraception has driven the U.S. abortion rate down to its lowest level

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.700
since the passage of Roe vs. Wade.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:29.400
And that number could be brought down even further by making the pill available over-the-counter.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:34.200
The major lesson of history is that activists should focus on changing individual minds.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.180
Because outlawing a widely accepted practice always leads to more human suffering.

